so the inciming message from the llama.cpp sould still be cut off or would it just keep streaming untilll the real end?

> Assistant:
Streaming will keep delivering tokens until the generation actually ends, but it will still stop at the same real limits.

So:

- if the model naturally finishes, streaming reaches the true end
- if `max_tokens` / `-n` is hit, streaming stops there
- if the server/model context budget forces a shorter effective answer, streaming still stops there
- if the model just produces an incomplete rewrite and ends, streaming shows that too

In other words:

- streaming changes how you receive the output
- it does not change how much output the model is allowed to produce

So for your case:

- if the current cut-off is caused by buffered-response timeout or UI waiting, streaming helps a lot
- if the current cut-off is caused by output cap or model limitations, streaming will still cut off at the same point, just progressively instead of all at once

The reliable way to think about it is:

- `stream: true` affects transport behavior
- `max_tokens` / `-n` affect generation length
- prompt/context quality affects whether the answer is useful before it stops

So no, streaming does not guarantee “the real end” if the model never gets permission or capacity to produce the full file.

