rsation history?"    }  ]}So the model gets just enough context, not the entire universe.The important architecture piece is a Context Pack Builder.User asks question  -> Detect current editor file/selection  -> Detect mentioned filenames/modules/functions  -> Search indexed repo chunks  -> Pull recent conversation summary  -> Pull last few exact messages  -> Pull pinned files if any  -> Build compact messages[]  -> Send to llama-serverYou want memory layers:Layer 1: Exact current context  Current open file, selected text, cursor area.  This is high priority.Layer 2: Recent chat  Last few user/assistant turns.  Keep exact text.Layer 3: Conversation summary  Older chat compressed into a running summary.Layer 4: File memory  One summary per file.  Example: "Editor.LLM.Client wraps llama-server /v1/chat/completions."Layer 5: File chunks  Searchable fragments by path, module, function, and text.Layer 6: Pinned context  Files the user explicitly says are important.The trick is that chat messages should eventually stop storing giant file blobs inline. Instead, store references:%{  role: "user",  content: "Can you update the streaming client?",  context_refs: [    %{type: :file_chunk, path: "lib/editor/llm/client.ex", lines: 20..80},    %{type: :file_summary, path: "lib/editor/chat/conversation.ex"}  ]}Then later, when building the prompt, editor_umbrella can decide whether to rehydrate those refs into actual text.For files, I would store something like:project_files  id  path  language  sha256  summary  symbols  last_seen_atfile_chunks  id  file_id  chunk_index  start_line  end_line  content  summary  embedding optional  sha256Then the model does not need to “remember what files are what.” The app does.The model only needs a compact map:Known relevant files:- lib/editor/llm/client.ex — HTTP client for llama-server- lib/editor/llm/stream.ex — SSE stream parser- lib/editor/chat.ex — chat context API- lib/editor_web/live/editor_live.ex — LiveView UI stateThat alone helps the LLM orient itself before seeing exact snippets.Also, llama-server KV cache or slots can help performance, but they do not solve this memory problem. Cache can reduce recomputation for repeated prompt prefixes. It does not magically give the model unlimited project memory. The clean solution is still: external memory + retrieval + compact prompt construction.For editor_umbrella, I would name the core piece something simple:Editor.ContextPackIts job:Editor.ContextPack.build(%{  conversation_id: conversation_id,  project_id: project_id,  user_message: message,  current_file: current_file,  selection: selection,  token_budget: 8192})And it returns:%{  messages: [...],  refs: [...],  estimated_tokens: 6200}That becomes the boundary between your editor cognition and llama-server.The big design rule:Never append everything.Always select, summarize, and rehydrate.That is how you beat the scale problem.

